OpenSSL 4.1.0: the inner server_name padding of an ECH ClientHello is 9 bytes short

Kuicksilver's own code, the fourth patch carried under ADR-0019 (docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md), applied after
openssl-4.1.0-ech-retry-configs-raw.patch; pinned by its SHA-256 in gradle/openssl.toml as patch_4, applied by
tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with the first OpenSSL release that fixes it. Known
upstream: issue openssl/openssl#32170, pull request #32930 (open) with the same one-line fix. Applies unchanged to OpenSSL
master at df4c3359.

The bug: RFC 9849 section 6.1.3: "If the ClientHelloInner contained a "server_name" extension with a name of length D,
add max(0, M - D) bytes of padding", M the ECHConfig's maximum_name_length. ossl_ech_calc_padding (ech_internal.c)
compares M with D + 9 (the length of a whole server_name extension), so it adds max(0, M - D - 9): names of length
M - 8 to M get no padding, and their length shows through the rounding to 32 bytes that follows.

The fix: compare M with D. The no-name case (M + 9, the length of a server_name extension with an M-byte name) and the
rounding to a multiple of 32 bytes (at least 128) stay as they are.

diff --git a/ssl/ech/ech_internal.c b/ssl/ech/ech_internal.c
--- a/ssl/ech/ech_internal.c
+++ b/ssl/ech/ech_internal.c
@@ -637,9 +637,12 @@
         return 0;
     mnl = ee->max_name_length;
     if (mnl != 0) {
-        /* do weirder padding if SNI present in inner */
+        /*
+         * RFC 9849, section 6.1.3: with a server_name of length D in the
+         * inner ClientHello, add max(0, M - D) bytes of padding
+         */
         if (s->ext.hostname != NULL) {
-            isnilen = strlen(s->ext.hostname) + 9;
+            isnilen = strlen(s->ext.hostname);
             innersnipadding = (mnl > isnilen) ? (int)(mnl - isnilen) : 0;
         } else {
             innersnipadding = (int)mnl + 9;
